                    insight_coherent:       `Plan made coherent: you handed back a surgical coherence map, repaired the positioning–pricing contradiction and a structural break while re-running the ripple, prioritised the highest-leverage realignment, kept the founders by deferring export rather than denying it, and resolved the committee's probe with a fix you actually built. The positioning, price, channel, resources and funding now point the same way — the committee can fund it.`,
                    insight_positioning:    `The core contradiction stayed live: premium positioning with a mass-market price was never reconciled, so the plan's centre — the link the red stamp was about — remained broken. Every other repair was overshadowed by the one the committee was always going to reject the plan over.`,
                    insight_pricing:        `You fixed in isolation: cutting the price without re-running margin → burn → runway → funding broke two decisions downstream. Margin fell to 26%, runway to three months, and the funding ask never moved to match. Strategic decisions are coupled — coherence must be re-verified after every repair.`,
                    insight_lowleverage:    `Low-leverage prioritisation: you spent your one realignment on a cosmetic fix while the contradiction carrying the most weight with the committee went untouched. Prioritisation has to target the broken link the decision-maker will actually reject the plan over.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A partial repair: some links were realigned, but the decisions didn't fully reinforce one another — enough residual contradiction for the committee to doubt whether the plan really hangs together.`,
                    insight_verdict:        `You opened with a verdict, not a diagnosis: declaring the plan weak without naming the contradicting decisions is exactly what the committee can't fund. Coherence work is surgical — locate the broken links, don't grade the wall.`,
                    insight_buyin:          `You lost founder buy-in: killing or avoiding the export dream may have been analytically clean, but a coherent plan the founders won't stand behind in the room dies just as surely as an incoherent one. Buy-in is a coherence constraint, not a soft add-on.`,
                    insight_masspivot:      `You made a coherent mass-market pivot: committing to mass-market and owning the consequences — margin to 26%, runway re-run, the funding ask moved — is a legitimate way to resolve the positioning–pricing contradiction. Incoherence comes from claiming both, not from choosing one honestly.`,
                    insight_shallow:        `Your diagnosis was thin: you didn't trace enough links to be sure you'd found the two contradictions that matter most. Coherence is a system property — a shallow map hides the break that sinks the raise.`,
